      "functional_impact_measured": "Surface labeling confirmed by N-terminal myc staining (L6). SNAP-GIPR constructs (L9/L10/L12/L13) show agonist-stimulated receptor trafficking (internalization following GIP(1-42) stimulation) in INS-1 832/3 beta cells, confirming surface expression and preserved receptor function. SNAPf-GIPR (L16/L17) confirmed at plasma membrane by cell-impermeant SNAP probe in AD293 cells.",
      "confidence": "high",
      "rationale": "The signal peptide spans residues 1\u201321 (Q is residue 21, the last SP residue); the mature N-terminus begins at R22. L6 explicitly states the myc-tag was inserted 'immediately downstream of the predicted signal peptide', which by the topology landmarks is insert_after_residue=21. Python confirms seq[20]='Q' and seq[21]='R'. The tag sits in the large extracellular N-terminal domain (ECD 22\u2013136), >115 residues from TM1 (137), and is sterically unconstrained. Six independent ledger lines (L6, L8, L9/L10, L16, L18) all place distinct tags at this same extracellular N-terminal junction with confirmed surface expression and, in the SNAP-GIPR studies, preserved agonist-stimulated trafficking. L6 (PMC4156404): full-length human GIPR expressed in HEK-293 cells with a myc-tag inserted immediately downstream of the predicted signal peptide (residues 1\u201321), placing the tag at the extracellular mature N-terminus (residue 22 onward). The same junction is independently confirmed by L8 (3\u00d7HA at N-terminus, internalization studies), L9/L10/L12/L13 (N-terminal SNAP-GIPR from Cisbio, stably expressed in HEK-293 and INS-1 832/3 cells), L16/L17 (N-terminal SNAPf replacing endogenous SP, AD293 cells), and L18 (FLAG+mNeonGreen at N-terminus, pcDNA constructs). [validation: surface_and_function; position: inferred; source: paper; cited_tag_residue: 21; entailment_verified: True]",
      "sources": [
        {
          "pmid": 25191754,
          "citation": "PMID 25191754",
          "claim": "Therefore, to label the receptors at their N-termini, a myc-tag was introduced immediately downstream of the predicted signal peptide."
        }
      ],
      "plddt": null,
